usage: elf-snapshot [-h] [--prefix PREFIX] [--namespace NAMESPACE]
                    [--module-name MODULE_NAME] [--max-total-bytes N]
                    [--include-stack]
                    binary [out_path]

Static ELF snapshot for RISC-V binaries (no debugger needed).

positional arguments:
  binary                Path to the RISC-V ELF binary
  out_path              Output path for <prefix>.v (default:
                        theories/LinuxProcessSem/<prefix>.v)

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  --prefix PREFIX       Prefix for generated file and definition names
                        (default: MemImage). Use SourceMemImage /
                        TargetMemImage for two-binary setups.
  --namespace NAMESPACE
                        Rocq logical namespace for shard imports (default:
                        LinuxProcessSemantics.LinuxProcessSem)
  --module-name MODULE_NAME
                        If set, append a `Module <name> <: MemImage` wrapper
                        exposing loaded_segments/init_pc/phdrs for the
                        Equivalence functor (e.g. Source or Target).
  --max-total-bytes N   Whole-image byte cap for this run (decimal, or 0x
                        hex). Outranks the ELF_SNAPSHOT_MAX_TOTAL_BYTES
                        environment variable, which outranks the built-in
                        MAX_TOTAL_BYTES (536870912). Refused above
                        MAX_TOTAL_BYTES_CEILING (2139095040), the largest
                        image this toolchain can coqchk.
  --include-stack       Include a synthetic stack segment (not yet
                        implemented)

Three other modes are dispatched before these options are parsed:
  elf_snapshot.py --rebuild <MemImage.v> <out-binary>
      write an ELF the kernel loads to exactly that snapshot's image
      (rebuild_elf says what is recovered; the result is held to a
      round trip through the parser above)
  elf_snapshot.py --print-phdrs <binary>
      print the binary's AT_PHDR/AT_PHENT/AT_PHNUM as `KEY VALUE`
      lines (compare them with what a real execve gives)
  elf_snapshot.py --verify <path>...
      parse-check every ELF under the given files/directories and
      write nothing (build gate; nonzero exit if any is rejected).
      Its report names the cap it applied and which source set it.

--max-total-bytes and the ELF_SNAPSHOT_MAX_TOTAL_BYTES environment
variable apply to those three modes too, so the build gate
verifies against the same cap the snapshot will run under.
